# MCP server configuration — installed verbatim to /etc/mcp/mcp.env by
# build-ops/vm/mcp-vm/mcp.sh.
#
# Sourced by the helper scripts in /usr/local/bin and read by the systemd units
# as an EnvironmentFile. This is the ONE place these values are written; change
# them here and push:
#
#   ansible-playbook playbooks/mcp-vm.yml --tags mcp
#
# which restarts the server and the gateway. No rebake, no VM replacement.
#
# NO SECRETS HERE, only the ids of secrets — this repo is public-safe. Values
# come from Secret Manager at run time.
#
# systemd's EnvironmentFile parser does NOT expand variables, so every path is
# spelled out in full rather than composed from the ones above it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paths. The split matters: the venv is baked into the image (replaced with it);
# everything stateful is under /data (kept across deploys).
#
# MCP_VENV is the image's contract — build-vm-images' install-mcp.sh creates it,
# and mcp.sh checks for it by the same path.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MCP_VENV=/opt/mcp/venv
MCP_DATA=/data
MCP_REPOS=/data/repos

# The service user's HOME. graphify's `global add` writes to
# Path.home()/".graphify" — hardcoded upstream, with no flag to redirect it — so
# HOME must be on the data disk or the merged graph silently lands on the boot
# disk and is lost the next time the image changes.
MCP_HOME=/data/mcp
MCP_GRAPH=/data/mcp/.graphify/global-graph.json

# Refresh watermark: the UTC timestamp of the last SUCCESSFUL run. Repos whose
# GitHub pushed_at is at or below this are skipped without being fetched.
MCP_STATE=/data/mcp/last-run

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# graphify itself. LOOPBACK NOW, and this is a real change: it used to bind
# 0.0.0.0:8080 and be the process the load balancer talked to. The OAuth gateway
# holds :8080 and graphify sits behind it, unreachable from outside the VM by any
# route at all.
#
# That is an independent containment layer on top of the firewall (tag `mcp`,
# Google's LB ranges only) and the API-key gate. It also means the API key below is
# no longer a user-facing credential — it is the loopback password between the two
# processes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MCP_BIND=127.0.0.1
MCP_PORT=8081
MCP_HTTP_PATH=/mcp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The OAuth gateway (mcp-auth). This is what the load balancer reaches.
#
# MCP_PUBLIC_URL and MCP_RESOURCE are the two values discovery is built from: the
# advertised redirect URI, the token issuer and the token audience are all derived
# from the first, and the second is the canonical resource URI published in
# /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource. Both must match what clients actually dial,
# scheme and host exactly, or discovery fails in a way that reads like a TLS problem.
#
# MCP_PUBLIC_URL + /auth/callback is also the ONE redirect URI registered on the
# Google OAuth client. Clients' own dynamic callbacks are handled behind it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MCP_AUTH_PORT=8080
MCP_PUBLIC_URL=https://mcp.deployza.com
MCP_RESOURCE=https://mcp.deployza.com/mcp

# Dynamic client registrations and encrypted upstream tokens. Under the service
# home so mcp-auth's ReadWritePaths= exception is a single directory.
MCP_AUTH_STATE=/data/mcp/auth

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who may use the server: anyone with a Google Workspace account in this domain.
# There is no group check and no allowlist.
#
# The real boundary is Google's own consent screen, set to Internal, which refuses
# any account outside this domain before a request reaches the VM. The gateway
# re-checks the domain on every request as defence in depth and to write the audit
# line — see WorkspaceDomainMiddleware in mcp_auth_app.py, which also records what
# this does NOT buy: no per-person revocation, and an issued token stays valid until
# it expires even after the account behind it is suspended.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MCP_ALLOWED_DOMAIN=deployza.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secret Manager secret ids, in the VM's own project. Values are never baked;
# they are fetched at run time and kept in process memory.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MCP_API_KEY_SECRET=mcp-api-key
# Renamed from mcp-github-pat: this PAT is now shared with website-vm's
# docs-refresh too, not mcp-only (build-terraform/builds/secrets.tf,
# build-vm-images/docs/apidocs-vm-build-plan.md). That rename needed image 1-2
# and a VM replacement because this file was baked then; it is pushed now.
MCP_GITHUB_PAT_SECRET=github-readonly-pat
MCP_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_SECRET=mcp-google-oauth-client-id
MCP_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET_SECRET=mcp-google-oauth-client-secret
MCP_JWT_KEY_SECRET=mcp-jwt-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What to index.
#
# Default is every non-archived repo the PAT can see in the org. The exclude list
# is space-separated and holds repos that cost a fetch and a scan but contribute
# almost nothing to the graph.
#
# `build-docs` WAS excluded here and no longer is. It was excluded because a
# docs-only repo parsed to nothing — which stopped being true when the refresh
# gained its Markdown pass (mcp-md-graph). Measured on the real repo: 7
# Markdown files, **63 nodes and 75 edges, 0 dangling links**, at zero tokens.
# Excluding it now would drop the single best-connected doc corpus in the org.
#
# `build-parent-pom` stays excluded: it is a single POM, and Maven dependencies
# are not modelled at all (build-terraform/docs/mcp.md §11, sibling repo).
# It has no Markdown to gain from.
#
# OPEN ITEM: this list is a starting guess, not a measured one. After the first
# full build, check `graphify global list` for repos contributing near-zero nodes
# and add them here. Re-check it after the Markdown pass has run a full org
# sweep — a repo that looked empty as code may now carry real doc nodes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MCP_GITHUB_ORG=deployza
MCP_EXCLUDE_REPOS="build-parent-pom"
